course: dictionary
lesson: 262
title: Always, and what it really means
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Always» is a small word with big jobs. Most of the time it means every time, with no exception: «We always subtract the smaller number from the bigger one.» If the rule always works, you can count on it.
explain: Look for «not always». In «a loud crack does not always mean a break», the word not tells you the crack sometimes is not a break. So always and not always are opposites in a useful way: one says every time, the other says not every time.
explain: «Always» can also mean again and again, often in an annoying way. «He is always losing his keys» does not mean he loses them every single minute. It means he loses them so often that it is annoying. Check the clues around the word to see which meaning fits.
explain: To test your meaning, swap in «every time» or «without exception». If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning. If it sounds silly, look again at the clues.
fact: Always means every time, with no exception.
fact: Always can mean forever, as in «I will always love you.»
fact: Always can mean again and again, often in an annoying way, as in «you're always nicking my stuff».
fact: «Not always» means it does not happen every time.
fact: «As always» means just as it happens every time, often said with a sigh. Formal writing uses words like «invariably», «without exception» or «at all times» instead.
exercise: «Water always boils at 100 °C at sea level.» What does always mean here? | For a short time | Sometimes, when it is windy | *Every time, with no exception | Only on hot days
exercise: «A loud crack does not always mean a break.» What does «not always» tell you? | It means a loud crack is always a break | Breaks are always silent | *A loud crack is sometimes not a break | Loud cracks are never heard
exercise: «He is always losing his keys.» Which meaning fits best? | Only once, and never again | *Again and again, often in an annoying way | Forever, for all time | Quietly, at night
test: «Always wear a helmet when you ride.» What is this safety rule saying? | Wear one if it is raining | *Wear one every time you ride, with no exception | Wear one only on Sundays | Wear one when you feel brave
test: In a formal report, which word could replace «always» in «The rule applies always»? | *invariably | sometimes | perhaps | quickly
exam: «The biggest pack is often cheaper per unit, but not always, so always do the sum.» What does the second always mean? | *Every time, with no exception | Once, at the start | Sometimes, when it is sunny | Only if you are bored
